## Why This Is Asked
This interview question tests dynamic programming with prefix-state tracking and solution reconstruction. The inner minimum calculation forces careful leftward scanning, providing a tangible beginner-to-intermediate challenge while remaining implementable in O(n²k) time.

## Key Concepts
- Dynamic programming over prefixes with block count tracking
- On-the-fly maintenance of minimum values over sliding windows
- Solution reconstruction via backpointers
- Time complexity: O(n²k), Space complexity: O(nk)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxValueBlocks(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const pre = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));

  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= Math.min(k, i); t++) {
      let currentMin = Infinity;
      for (let p = i; p >= t; p--) {
        currentMin = Math.min(currentMin, A[p-1]);
        const candidate = (dp[p-1][t-1] + currentMin * (i-p+1));
        if (candidate > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = candidate;
          pre[i][t] = p;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruct solution
  const blocks = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (t > 0) {
    const p = pre[i][t];
    blocks.unshift([p, i]);
    i = p - 1;
    t--;
  }

  return { maxValue: dp[n][k], blocks };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
DP over subsequences with a fixed length captures real-world problems like quantizing sequences while minimizing total variation; it tests state definition, reconstruction, and complexity trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- DP state: last index and length; - Transition: O(n) over prior indices; - Reconstruction via parent pointers; - Complexity: O(n^2 k) time, O(nk) space.

## Code Example
```javascript
function minCostSubsequence(A, k) {
  const n = A.length, INF = 1e18;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(INF));
  const par = Array.from({length: n}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for (let i=0;i<n;i++) dp[i][1] = 0;
  for (let t=2;t<=k;t++) {
    for (let i=0;i<n;i++) {
      for (let j=0;j<i;j++) {
        const val = dp[j][t-1] + Math.abs(A[i]-A[j]);
        if (val < dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = val; par[i][t] = j; }
      }
    }
  }
  let best = INF, last = -1;
  for (let i=0;i<n;i++) if (dp[i][k] < best) { best = dp[i][k]; last = i; }
  const idx = Array(k);
  let t = k, cur = last;
  while (t>=1) { idx[t-1] = cur; cur = par[cur][t]; t--; }
  return {cost: best, indices: idx};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a three-dimensional DP that models constrained path planning with a binary obstacle grid and a budget of unblocks. Requires careful state definition, transitions, and path reconstruction. 

## Key Concepts
- DP with an extra dimension for unblocks (t)
- Handling blocked vs unblocked cells in transitions
- Path reconstruction from 3D DP table
- Time/space trade-offs and rolling optimization

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxPath(grid, blocked, k) {
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length;
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array.from({length: m}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG)));
  if (!blocked[0][0]) dp[0][0][0] = grid[0][0];
  else if (k > 0) dp[0][0][1] = grid[0][0];
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < m; j++) {
      for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
        const val = grid[i][j];
        if (i > 0) {
          dp[i][j][t] = Math.max(dp[i][j][t], dp[i-1][j][t] + val * (blocked[i][j] ? 0 : 1));
          if (blocked[i][j] && t > 0) dp[i][j][t] = Math.max(dp[i][j][t], dp[i-1][j][t-1] + val);
        }
        if (j > 0) {
          dp[i][j][t] = Math.max(dp[i][j][t], dp[i][j-1][t] + val * (blocked[i][j] ? 0 : 1));
          if (blocked[i][j] && t > 0) dp[i][j][t] = Math.max(dp[i][j][t], dp[i][j-1][t-1] + val);
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return dp[n-1][m-1][0];
}
```

